            <ArticleTitle>Suture-induced right coronary artery stenosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>215-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jcct.2010.01.005</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>An 82-year-old patient developed right heart failure in the days after surgical aortic valve replacement. Coronary CT angiography showed a high-grade stenosis of the mid-right coronary artery. Adjacent suture material seen on noncontrast CT suggested that the lesion was related to surgical closure of the right atrial cannulation site. Invasive angiography confirmed the stenosis, and percutaneous intervention was successfully performed.</AbstractText>
